# =============================================================================
# run_lightning.config - LIGHTNING-driven training runbook
# =============================================================================
#
# What this file is
# -----------------
# A documentation-only, comment-only walkthrough of the full pipeline when
# you want sample selection driven by lightning activity. Every command
# here is meant to be copy-pasted (without the leading `# `) from the
# project root.
#
# Track recap: the "lightning track" gates the training sample list by
# the per-map occurrence-fraction threshold computed inside
# identify_lightning_periods.py - the model only sees timesteps where
# lightning was meaningfully active, which produces a smaller but
# higher-signal dataset. For OPERA-driven sample selection, see
# `run_opera.config` instead.
#
# Notation in the parallelism notes:
#   [single]  single-threaded, CPU-bound
#   [mp:N]    Python multiprocessing pool of up to N workers
#   [GPU]     CUDA / cuDNN / mixed-precision tensor cores
#   [I/O]     network / disk bound (SFTP, large file transfers)
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 0 - Configure cadence  [single, ~1 second]
# =============================================================================
#
# Writes our_data/timestep_config.json. Single source of truth for every
# downstream script. Re-run ONLY when you edit product_cadences.config.
#
#     python validate_timestep.py --step_minutes 15
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 1 - Arrange raw lightning KMLs  [single, ~1 minute]
# =============================================================================
#
# Renames the dump-format KMLs into kml_data/YYYY-MM-DD/YYYY-MM-DD.kml.
# Resumable: existing date dirs are left alone unless --force is added.
#
#     python our_data/raw_data/lightning_arrange.py \
#         -s our_data/raw_data/lightning \
#         --start-date 2025-04-01 --end-date 2025-09-30
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 2 - Acquire satellite + radar inputs (per product)  [I/O]
# =============================================================================
#
# The model needs MTG and OPERA as inputs even when lightning drives
# the *sample selection*. Each pipeline filters in
# `pipeline_<product>.py` BEFORE transfer based on
# `products.<product>.filter` so only the cadence-aligned files cross
# the wire.
#
# MTG FCI:
#     python our_data/satellite_data/pipeline_msg_mtg.py \
#         -s 2025/04/01-0000 -e 2025/09/30-2350 \
#         --password_file password.txt \
#         --products_file our_data/satellite_data/satellite_products.json
#
# OPERA radar:
#     python our_data/opera_data/pipeline_opera.py \
#         -s 2025/04/01-0000 -e 2025/09/30-2350 \
#         --password_file password.txt
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 3 - Generate lightning .npy maps  [mp:16, resumable]
# =============================================================================
#
# read_kml_version2.py iterates dates sequentially but fans out .npy
# writes across up to 16 worker processes (Windows handle limit). KML
# parsing per date is single-threaded.
#
# Cadence: 10-minute filter-aligned (96 maps/day) keyed to
# products.lightning.filter. Variable-width windows ensure every stroke
# in the day lands in exactly one bin.
#
# Resume support: already-complete dates (all 3 sub-products full) are
# skipped. Pass --force to overwrite.
#
#     python our_data/lightning_data/read_kml_version2.py
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 4 - Reproject satellite + OPERA to Romania grid  [mp:6 (--workers)]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reprojects each product through a precomputed KD-tree mapping. Lightning
# is already on the grid (read_kml writes directly) so no reproject step
# is needed for it.
#
# Per-category logs land at our_data/reprojected_data/reproject_<cat>.log
# and are aggregated into our_data/reprojected_data/errors.txt after
# every run (so intersect_product_coverage.py can consume a single file).
#
#     python reproject.py --all --workers 12
#
# Or per-category if you only need one:
#     python reproject.py --satellite MTG --workers 12
#     python reproject.py --opera --workers 12
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 5 - Per-product coverage summaries  [single, fast]
# =============================================================================
#
# All emit <product>_summary.csv at project root. MTG and OPERA also
# emit <product>_missing_timesteps.json; lightning emits
# lightning_active_steps.csv instead (per-map activity flags, used by
# intersect as a presence gate).
#
#     python our_data/satellite_data/summarize_mtg.py
#     python our_data/opera_data/summarize_opera_data.py
#     python our_data/lightning_data/summarize_lightning_data.py
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 6 - Per-patch activity index (LIGHTNING)  [single, fast]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reads lightning_active_steps.csv, computes per-map occurrence-fraction,
# applies threshold = 0.05 * mean(fractions over active maps), and writes
# our_data/lightning_periods/lightning_patches.csv keyed by MASTER HHMM
# (the snap target so it lines up with the master grid every other
# product walks).
#
#     python identify_lightning_periods.py
#
# Optional diagnostics:
#     python our_data/lightning_data/visualize_lightning_stats.py \
#         --output_dir our_data/lightning_data
#     python lightning_fraction.py   # active-scoped fraction prior used by
#                                    # train_models.py for focal loss
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 7 - Cross-product manifest  [single, fast]
# =============================================================================
#
# Intersects all product gates AND the lightning active-steps CSV. Slot
# survives iff every product has data AND lightning's occurrence flag is
# 1 there. Output: our_data/timestep_manifest.csv + intersect_summary.png.
#
#     python intersect_product_coverage.py \
#         --summary  mtg=mtg_summary.csv \
#         --summary  opera=opera_summary.csv \
#         --summary  lightning=lightning_summary.csv \
#         --active   lightning=lightning_active_steps.csv \
#         --errors_log our_data/reprojected_data/errors.txt
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 8 - Continuous sequences + Czibula split (LIGHTNING source)
#          [single, fast]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reads lightning_patches.csv as the per-(date, time) patch-activity
# index, intersects with the manifest (final cross-product gate), and
# writes per-source CSVs:
#     our_data/train_data_lightning.csv
#     our_data/validation_data_lightning.csv
#     our_data/test_data_lightning.csv
#     our_data/sequence_meta_lightning.json
# Plus an audit CSV listing every (date, time) the manifest dropped:
#     our_data/extract_patch_seq_drops_lightning.csv
#
#     python extract_patch_seq_for_datasets.py --source lightning
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 9 - Extract patch arrays from reprojected data  [mp]
# =============================================================================
#
# Walks the union of (date, time) tuples across
# train/val/test_data_lightning.csv (Step 8's output - already manifest-
# filtered), looks up the active patches at each step from
# lightning_patches.csv, and slices 256x256 patches from each
# reprojected product. Resumable: already-written .npy files are
# skipped on rerun.
#
#     python extract_patches.py --source lightning
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 10 - Per-variable normalization stats  [single, optional GPU via CuPy]
# =============================================================================
#
# Mean/std per variable computed on the TRAIN split for this source
# only (reads train_data_lightning.csv + sequence_meta_lightning.json,
# snaps to per-product filters, writes
# our_data/normalization_stats_lightning.json). The CuPy GPU path
# kicks in automatically when cupy is importable.
#
#     python compute_normalization_stats.py --source lightning
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 11 - Build TFRecord datasets (LIGHTNING source)  [single, I/O-bound]
# =============================================================================
#
# Reads sequence_meta_lightning.json + the per-source train/val/test
# CSVs. Writes sharded TFRecord files to our_data/datasets/<mode>_lightning/
# along with a per-shard metadata.json that train_models reconstructs
# the parse signature from.
#
# Canonical lightning-track mode is `mtg_lightning` (target: occurrence).
# `mtg_radar` and `mtg_radar_continuous` also work if you prefer the RZC
# label.
#
#     python create_datasets.py --mode mtg_lightning --source lightning
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 12 - TRAIN MODEL  [GPU, mixed precision fp16]
# =============================================================================
#
# ### CUT-OFF: two paths from here ###############################
#
# Path A (SIMPLE, recommended for a first run): train the standard
# coalition model and stop. One single-output forecaster, no transformer
# head, same architecture train_models has shipped with all along.
#
#     python train_models.py \
#         --config training.config \
#         --mode mtg_lightning \
#         --source lightning \
#         --stage base
#
# Path B (DOMAIN ADAPTATION with Swin transformer head): run the base
# stage as above, then in the SAME process load it, freeze the backbone,
# graft a 2-block Swin head (8x8 windowed attention + 3 per-lead-time
# projection heads) on top, and fine-tune that head with AdamW. All
# hyperparameters come from the [finetune] section of training.config.
#
#     python train_models.py \
#         --config training.config \
#         --mode mtg_lightning \
#         --source lightning \
#         --stage both
#
# Both stages save under ./models/ with `_<mode>_<source>` suffixes:
#     coalition_mtg_lightning_lightning.keras           (base)
#     coalition_mtg_lightning_lightning_finetuned.keras (Swin head)
#     history_mtg_lightning_lightning.json
#     history_mtg_lightning_lightning_finetuned.json
#     checkpoints/*_latest.keras                        (resumable per-epoch)
#
# Resume support: each stage writes its own per-epoch checkpoint so a
# crash mid-run only loses the partial epoch. Pass --fresh to force a
# clean start.
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Step 13 - Evaluate  [GPU]
# =============================================================================
#
# For the BASE model:
#     python evaluate_coalition.py
#     python bundle_eval_scores.py
#
# For the FINE-TUNED model: point evaluate_coalition.py at
# coalition_<run_tag>_finetuned.keras instead of the base checkpoint
# (the metadata.json schema is the same so no other change is needed).
#
#
# =============================================================================
# Notes
# =============================================================================
#
# - The lightning-track dataset is typically smaller than the OPERA-track
#   one (filtered by both timestep_config and the 0.30*mean occurrence
#   threshold). The Swin head's head_dropout = 0.1 default is sized for
#   this; bump it up if you see overfitting in the fine-tune history.
#
# - The Swin head treats the backbone's output (B, 3, 256, 256, 32) as
#   ONE shared feature representation, then projects to 3 lead-time
#   outputs via independent 1x1 convs. The 3 future steps share gradient
#   signal through the Swin trunk; the per-step heads are intentionally
#   tiny.
#
# - If you ever change --step_minutes in step 0, you must re-run
#   read_kml_version2.py (the .npy files are at the OLD cadence) and
#   every step after it. The on-disk filter-aligned filenames are
#   incompatible across step-minute changes.
